  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=3,$ then what is $5\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+12\\pi )?$",
  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+2\\cdot 6\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+12\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=3,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+12\\pi )=3.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+12\\pi )=5\\cdot 3=15.\n$$",
